last month; # The 1-2-3 Conjecture for edge sum-labellings ... The conjecture was raised by Karoński, Łuczak, and Thomason in 2004 and was recently solved by Keusch, so its database status is solved.\n\n# The 1-2-3 Conjecture for edge sum-labellings\n\nAbout 1 year old · traced to 2025\n\nLet $G$G be a simple graph. For a positive integer $k$k, an edge $k$k-labelling is a map $\\ell:E(G)\\to\\{1,\\dots,k\\}$ℓ:E(G)→{1,…,k}. For each vertex $u$u, define its incident sum by\n\n$$\\sigma_\\ell(u)=\\sum_{v\\in N(u)}\\ell(uv).$$σ ℓ​(u)=v∈N(u)∑​ℓ(uv).\n\nThe labelling is sum-proper when adjacent vertices have distinct incident sums, and $\\chi^e_{\\rm S}(G)$χ S e​(G) is the least such $k$k, when it exists. The 1-2-3 Conjecture. If $G$G is connected and $G\\neq K_2$G=K 2​, then\n\n$$\\chi^e_{\\rm S}(G)\\leq 3.$$χ S e​(G)≤3.\n\nThe conjecture was raised by Karoński, Łuczak, and Thomason in 2004 and was recently solved by Keusch, so its database status is solved.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nJulien Bensmail, Beatriz Martins and Chaoliang Tang, “1-2 Conjectures for Graphs with Low Degeneracy Properties”, arXiv:2504.21452 (2025).\n\ngraph-coloringgraph-labeling\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBounding the distant irregularity strength of graphs via a non-uniformly biased random weight assignment (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0195669824000465)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Later Keusch [31] proved that weights 1,2,3,4$1,2,3,4$ always suffice and very recently he announced [30] to finally resolve the conjecture in the affirmative.\nOut of the vastness of these, one of the most well known offspring of the irregularity strength is its variant within which one confines to requiring distinct weighted degrees only for adjacent vertices. This concept was introduced in 2004 by Karoński, Łuczak and Thomason [26], together with a very appealing conjecture that merely weights 1$1$, 2$2$ and 3$3$ are sufficient for all graphs without isolated edges within such a setting. This so-called 1–2–3 Conjecture is yet another central problem of this field, which gained no less attention than the conjecture of Faudree and Lehel, see e.g. [27], [28], [29], [30], [31], [32], [33], [34] for general bounds, [26], [35], [36], [37], [38] – for more specific results or [39], [40], [41], [42] – for strong list generalisations. In particular, in 2021 the 1–2–3 Conjecture was confirmed for regular graphs with large enough degrees [32], and in 2022 — for general graphs with minimum degree \\delta =\\Omega (log\\Delta )$\\delta =\\Omega (log\\Delta )$ [33], where \\Delta $\\Delta $ is the maximum degree of G$G$. Later Keusch [31] proved that weights 1,2,3,4$1,2,3,4$ always suffice and very recently he announced [30] to finally resolve the conjecture in the affirmative.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn decomposing multigraphs into locally irregular submultigraphs (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300323002187)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Finally, the 1-2-3 Conjecture was confirmed by Keusch in [9].\nFor many years the best known general result about this conjecture was that every graph containing no isolated edges admits {\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)\\le 5${\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)\\le 5$ and was proved by Kalkowski, Karoński and Pfender in [7]. There were also some other results about the 1-2-3 Conjecture and we refer the reader to the survey [16]. Recently, the conjecture has been intensively investigated and in case of regular graphs Przybyło proved in [13] that every d$d$-regular graph G$G$, where d\\ge 2$d\\ge 2$, admits {\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)\\le 4${\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)\\le 4$ and if d\\ge {10}^{8}$d\\ge {10}^{8}$ then G$G$ admits {\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)\\le 3${\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)\\le 3$. Then Keusch proved in [10] that every graph containing no isolated edges admits {\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)\\le 4${\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)\\le 4$. Finally, the 1-2-3 Conjecture was confirmed by Keusch in [9].\n\nA weaker version of this neighbor distinguishing edge coloring is multiset neighbor distinguishing edge coloring. For each vertex x$x$ from G$G$ we denote by M\\left(x\\right):=\\left[\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}f\\right(e):x\\in e]$M\\left(x\\right):=\\left[\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}f\\right(e):x\\in e]$ the multiset of colors of edges incident to the vertex x$x$. In this coloring two adjacent vertices x$x$ and y$y$ are distinguished if M\\left(x\\right)\\ne M\\left(y\\right)$M\\left(x\\right)\\ne M\\left(y\\right)$. We define a parameter {\\chi }_{M}\\left(G\\right)${\\chi }_{M}\\left(G\\right)$ as the smallest k$k$ for which there exists a multiset neighbor distinguishing edge coloring of G$G$. We can easily see that every graph G$G$ satisfies {\\chi }_{M}\\left(G\\right)\\le {\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)${\\chi }_{M}\\left(G\\right)\\le {\\chi }_{\\Sigma }\\left(G\\right)$ because if the sums are different then the multisets are also different. The best known result about the multiset neighbor distinguishing edge coloring is the following theorem which was proved by Vučković in [17].\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTheorem of the Day (https://www.theoremoftheday.org/Resources/TheoremNotes.htm)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Image: Theorem under Construction icon 